Gambling disorder
Mental Health & Substance Use
Preoccupation with gambling, Chasing losses, Lying to conceal gambling

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MHS-025
Reason for Visit: Severe stress-induced tension headaches, insomnia, and vomiting triggered by escalating, uncontrollable sports betting debts.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted severe, unremitting head pain, three days of insomnia, and acute vomiting causing dehydration, entirely precipitated by extreme financial distress from gambling. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to rule out acute intracranial pathology (e.g., hemorrhage) given the severity of the headache, manage dehydration, and provide psychological stabilization prior to routine clinic availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My head is splitting open, I haven't slept in three days, and I keep throwing up. The debt collectors are calling, and I can't stop placing bets to try and win it back."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports severe, band-like tension headaches and intractable insomnia worsening over the past 72 hours, culminating in stress-induced vomiting today. The underlying cause is a severe gambling disorder. The patient describes a frequent preoccupation with gambling, constantly planning the next venture. There is a compulsive need to gamble with increasing amounts of money to achieve the desired excitement, and profound restlessness or irritability when attempting to cut down or stop. The patient reports repeated unsuccessful efforts to control or stop gambling, and admits that gambling is often initiated when feeling distressed, helpless, or guilty. The current crisis is driven by chasing losses by returning another day to get even, resulting in overwhelming debt. The patient is lying to conceal the extent of involvement with gambling from family. This behavior is jeopardizing their educational opportunity (failing university courses) and forcing them to rely on others to provide money to relieve desperate financial situations.
Functional Status: Currently incapacitated by severe headache and anxiety; neglecting university studies.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of accumulating dangerous debts; moderate risk of impulsive self-harm due to desperation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/90 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Visibly exhausted, clutching head, pale, diaphoretic.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals stress-induced tachycardia. Neurological exam shows no focal deficits, no photophobia, neck is supple (Kernig/Brudzinski negative), ruling out meningism. Fundoscopy reveals sharp optic discs with no papilledema. Abdomen is soft with hyperactive bowel sounds. Musculoskeletal exam shows severe tension and spasm in the bilateral trapezius and occipital musculature. The presentation strongly aligns with extreme somatic manifestation of psychological distress (preoccupation with gambling, chasing losses, lying).
Environmental Observations: Multiple screens open to various sports betting websites; empty energy drink cans piled on the desk.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Gambling disorder - Status: Exacerbated (Severe somatic symptom manifestation)
Differentials:

Subarachnoid Hemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely due to the gradual onset of the headache over three days, absence of a "thunderclap" character, supple neck, and normal neurological exam.

Migraine without Aura - Ruled out/unlikely due to the bilateral, band-like pressure nature of the pain (rather than unilateral/pulsating) and the absence of prominent photophobia/phonophobia.

Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the vomiting being directly correlated with spikes in severe anxiety regarding debt, absent diarrhea, and absent fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe somatic symptoms—intractable headache, insomnia, and vomiting—secondary to the extreme psychological distress of a severe gambling disorder. Telephone triage appropriately identified the severe headache as a potential red flag requiring physical assessment. Urgent after-hours medical evaluation was mandatory to conduct a neurological examination to rule out acute intracranial pathology and to assess hydration status. Confirming the somatic nature of the symptoms allowed for targeted, conservative treatment (antiemetics and simple analgesia) and urgent psychological intervention, avoiding an unnecessary and stressful emergency department visit while mitigating the severe distress.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Ondansetron 4mg to control vomiting and allow for oral rehydration. Prescribed oral Naproxen 500mg for the tension headache. Expected investigation results: South Oaks Gambling Screen yielding a score indicative of probable pathological gambling.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement "Website Blocking." Instruct the patient to immediately install gambling blocking software (e.g., BetBlocker) on all devices during the visit. Reward this immediate action with the relief that they have physically prevented themselves from worsening the debt tonight, lowering the acute anxiety driving the physical symptoms. This uses environmental restriction to enforce behavioral cessation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to specialized gambling addiction counseling services.
DME/Safety (Optional): Nil.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to monitor physical symptoms and confirm engagement with addiction services.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the headache suddenly changes to the worst pain ever experienced, if visual changes or weakness in an arm/leg occur, or if thoughts of self-harm escalate to a specific plan.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute physical symptoms treated; the underlying addiction requires specialized, long-term intervention.
Clinical Course: The headache and vomiting will improve rapidly with the medication and by halting the immediate gambling behavior. The financial and psychological recovery will be prolonged.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you cannot keep any fluids down for the next 12 hours, or if your headache becomes sudden and explosive, seek emergency medical care.

